  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA stationary front is currently located just south of\nKansas/Nebraska border. Strong to severe thunderstorms have \ndeveloped near this front this evening. Weaker showers and \nthunderstorms have also begun developing north of the front in a\nregime of increasing isentropic ascent. While the front remains\nstationary, rain chances slowly spread north and east towards \nthe Interstate 80 corridor through Friday morning. This is when\nrain chances peak. Showers and a few thunderstorms are expected\ninto Friday afternoon, but these should be widely scattered, \nkeeping rain chances lower. This activity is associated with a \nlow pressure system that will lift from Northern Mexico and the\nSouthern Rockies into the Central Plains. Cooler temperatures \nlinger behind the front, but cloud cover keeps temperatures from\nfalling lower. Expect 40s to low 50s to start Friday before \nslowly warming into the upper 50s and low 60s by afternoon. \nSlightly warmer temperatures in the upper 60s and lower 70s \nreside across Southeast Nebraska nearest the stationary front. \n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system moves over the area \nFriday night into Saturday. This system will support lingering \nshowers and patchy fog across the area as we remain north of \nthe front and maintain isentropic ascent. That said, rain \nshould be more hit or miss, with dry periods likely. By \nevening, the trend remains drier for Halloween festivities, but \na few light showers can't be entirely ruled out. With the \ncontinued rain and clouds, temperatures similar to Friday can be\nexpected on Saturday. Our next weather system remains hot on \nthe heels of our current system. As a result of this, cloud \ncover and low rain chances now linger into Sunday. Temperatures \ncontinue to be similar to previous days also. \n\nEarly next week sees a strong area of low pressure swing across\nthe Great Basin into the Central Plains. Cyclogenesis is \nanticipated west of the region with the current guidance moving\nthe developing area of low pressure directly over our area. In\nthis scenario, temperatures remain similar to previous days.\nWidespread rain chances are to be expected, making for a damp\nstart to the week. Rainfall could be heavy at times due to \nabove normal precipitable water values from modified tropical \nmoisture feeding into this system. Many locations could see \nstorm total rainfall amounts surpass one inch. At this time, \nthunderstorm chances remain lower with severe weather unlikely, \nbut this will need to be watched carefully as a shift to the \nnorth could change this forecast.\n\nWidespread rain chances continue into Tuesday before the weather\nsystem begins to shift east of the area. A strong cold front\nbegins to push across the area during this time. Gusty northwest\nwinds are possible behind the front as a result of the strong \narea of low pressure. Cooler temperatures begin to filter in as\nwell. Rain chances and gusty winds may linger into Wednesday, \nbut these should gradually diminish as everything moves off the \nthe east. By the end of the week, cooler temperatures, mainly in\nthe 50s, are expected. At least low rain chances linger during \nthis time as well.",
